 ],
 "caption_en": "Every grey dot on this map is 10,000 people who never went anywhere.\n\nWhere did Europe's Jews go? 🕯️\n\n1933: about 9.5 million Jews lived in Europe. 1950: about 3.5 million.\nPoland: over 3,000,000 to about 45,000. Germany: 525,000 to 37,000. Hungary: 445,000 to 190,000. Romania: nearly 757,000 (1930) to about 280,000. Czechoslovakia: 357,000 to 17,000. Austria: 191,000 to 18,000. Greece: 73,000 to 7,000. Yugoslavia: 70,000 to 3,500. Bulgaria: 50,000 to 6,500. Italy: 48,000 to 35,000. France: 250,000 to 235,000. Britain: 300,000 to about 450,000. The Soviet Union (European part 2,525,000 in 1933) still had some 2 million Jews in 1950.\nAbout 136,000 Jewish displaced persons settled in Israel, and over 80,000 in the United States by 1952.\nSix million Jews were murdered in the Holocaust.\n\n1 dot = 10,000 people, 13 countries, rounded. Dots are placed at each country's centre, not where people lived. The Soviet Union's 1950 dots are the whole Soviet Union.\n\nSources: United States Holocaust Memorial Museum (USHMM), Holocaust Encyclopedia: \"Jewish Population of Europe in 1933\", \"Remaining Jewish Population of Europe in 1945\", \"Displaced Persons\", based on the American Jewish Year Book. Photos: Warsaw 1934, Willem van de Poll, Nationaal Archief (CC0); the Great Synagogue of Warsaw, before 1939 (PD, destroyed 1943); the Exodus at Haifa, 1947, Fritz Cohen, GPO (PD). Map: Natural Earth (PD). Made with AI tools, AI voice (Noam's cloned voice), follow line in Noam's own voice.",
 "caption_he": "לאן נעלמו יהודי אירופה? ב 1933 חיו באירופה כ 9.5 מיליון יהודים. ב 1950 נותרו כ 3.5 מיליון. בפולין: משלושה מיליון לכ 45 אלף. כ 136 אלף ניצולים בנו את חייהם מחדש בישראל, ויותר מ 80 אלף בארצות הברית. הנקודות האפורות לא הלכו לשום מקום: שישה מיליון יהודים נרצחו בשואה.",
 "hashtags": [
  "#Holocaust",
  "#JewishHistory",
  "#NeverForget"
 ],
